Transitive verbs like saw increase the number of syntactic and semantic arguments of intransitives by one. To analyze the sentence in (1),

Sam saw Frodo.

we can assign a lexical entry like the one in (2) to this verb.

\(⟨\textit{saw}, (λx.(λy.\ct{see}(y, x)))⟩ ⊢ ((np\backslash s)/np)\)

In this case, we have a function of the following type:
\[(λx.(λy.\ct{see}(y, x))) : (e → (e → t))\]
This function is defined in terms of a relation on entities, \(\ct{see}\). That is, given two entities \(x\) and \(y\), \(\ct{see}(y, x)\) is some truth value.
Then, given the lexical entry for Sam on which its interpretation is \(\ct{s}\), we can derive the meaning and syntactic category of the sentence in (1):
\[\begin{prooftree}
\AxiomC{\(⟨\textit{Sam}, \ct{s}⟩ ⊢ np\)}
\AxiomC{\(⟨\textit{saw}, (λx.(λy.\ct{see}(y, x)))⟩ ⊢ (np\backslash s)/np\)}
\AxiomC{\(⟨\textit{Frodo}, \ct{f}⟩ ⊢ np\)}
\RightLabel{\(/\)}\BinaryInfC{\(⟨\textit{saw Frodo}, (λx.(λy.\ct{see}(y, x)))(\ct{f})⟩ ⊢ (np\backslash s)\)}
\RightLabel{\(\backslash\)}\BinaryInfC{\(⟨\textit{Sam saw Frodo}, (λx.(λy.\ct{see}(y, x)))(\ct{f})(\ct{s})⟩ ⊢ s\)}
\end{prooftree}\]
Since \((λx.(λy.\ct{see}(y, x)))\) takes two entities in order, we can obtain a function that takes one entity by applying it first to \(\ct{f}\):
\[(λx.(λy.\ct{see}(y, x)))(\ct{f})\] \[⇒ (λy.\ct{see}(y, \ct{f}))\]
The resulting function is a characteristic function of a set of entities. Thus if we apply the original function to two entities, we get a truth value:
\[(λx.(λy.\ct{see}(y, x)))(\ct{f})(\ct{s})\] \[⇒ (λy.\ct{see}(y, \ct{f}))(\ct{s})\] \[⇒ \ct{see}(\ct{s}, \ct{f})\]
This result, \(\ct{see}(\ct{s}, \ct{f})\), will be \(\true\) or \(\false\), depending on the model.

