In Syntactic categories, we said that intransitive verbs have the syntactic category \((np\backslash s)\): they take a noun phrase to their left, and together with it, they produce a sentence. And because they have this category, they have the semantic type \((e → t)\): they take an entity as their semantic argument, and they produce a truth value—that is, they are interpreted as characteristic functions.
Take the verb slept as an example.

Frodo slept.

To derive the sentence in (1), we can assign Frodo and slept the lexical entries in (2).

\(⟨\textit{Frodo}, \ct{f}⟩ ⊢ np\)  \(⟨\textit{slept}, (λx.\ct{sleep}(x))⟩ ⊢ (np\backslash s)\)

As usual, \(\ct{f}\) is an entity—some element of the domain of whatever model we happen to be considering: \[\ct{f} ∈ D_{e}\] Meanwhile, \((λx.\ct{sleep}(x))\) is a characteristic function of a set of elements of this very same domain: \[(λx.\ct{sleep}(x)) : (e → t)\] It takes an entity, and it gives you back a truth value (\(\true\) or \(\false\)).
Note the notation being used here: ‘\((λx.\ct{sleep}(x))\)’. This is known as λ-notation. It allows one to write functions down anonymously—i.e., without giving them names like \(g_{\textit{slept}}\). By using this notation, we are able to describe any given function’s behavior simply in the way that we notate it. In the case of \((λx.\ct{sleep}(x))\), we are saying that the function first takes an argument (\(x\)) and returns a truth value—either \(\true\) or \(\false\), depending on whether or not \(x\) slept.
Exactly which characteristic function is \(\ct{sleep}\)? We are not actually specifying this one way or the other. Instead, we are using the expression ‘\(\ct{sleep}\)’ to stand for \(I_{\mathcal{M}}(\textit{slept})\), for whatever the model \(\mathcal{M}\) happens to be.
Using the lexical entries for Frodo and slept, we can derive the sentence in (1):
\[\begin{prooftree}
\AxiomC{\(⟨\textit{Frodo}, \ct{f}⟩ ⊢ np\)}
\AxiomC{\(⟨\textit{slept}, (λx.\ct{sleep}(x))⟩ ⊢ (np\backslash s)\)}
\RightLabel{\(\backslash\)}\BinaryInfC{\(⟨\textit{Frodo slept}, (λx.\ct{sleep}(x))(\ct{f})⟩ ⊢ s\)}
\end{prooftree}\] As we’d expect, given the semantic type of \(s\) (i.e., \(t\)), the meaning derived for the entire sentence,
\[(λx.\ct{sleep}(x))(\ct{f})\]
is a truth value—\(\true\) or \(\false\), depending on which of these values \(\ct{sleep}\) maps \(\ct{f}\) to. To see what this meaning is more clearly, we can use β-reduction to substitute the argument \(\ct{f}\) in for the variable \(x\) inside the function:
\[(λx.\ct{sleep}(x))(\ct{f})\] \[⇒ \ct{sleep}(\ct{f})\]

