Let’s start with semantic types.
We’ll have two basic (or atomic) types: \(e\) and \(t\). Recall that \(e\) is the type of entities, or elements of the domain \(D\), for whatever model \(\mathcal{M}\) we happen to be using to interpret English.
Crucially, \(e\) is the semantic type of noun phrases like Jo and Bo. This just amounts to saying that noun phrases take their interpretations from the domain of the type \(e\): noun phrases are interpreted in \(D\), the domain of the model.
Meanwhile, \(t\) is the type of truth values, \(\true\) and \(\false\). In turn, the “domain” of type \(t\) is the set \(\{\true, \false\}\). And \(t\) is the semantic type of sentences like Jo jumps and Bo runs—expressions that can be true or false in some model.
For either of these semantic types, we can refer to its domain—the set of possible meanings of that type—using the notation ‘\(D_{e}\)’ or ‘\(D_{t}\)’. That is, we can write the domain of semantic type \(e\) as ‘\(D_{e}\)’, and we can write the domain of semantic type \(t\) as ‘\(D_{t}\)’. Thus \(D_{e}\) is just \(D\) (the domain of the model), while \(D_{t}\) is \(\{\true, \false\}\) (the set of truth values).
To summarize, we have the following correspondence:
But we would like to be able to have semantic types not just for noun phrases and sentences, but for any syntactic category that an expression might possibly have. So let’s use our two atomic types \(e\) and \(t\) to generate a whole collection of types that we can pull from.
Here is how we can do this. In addition to \(e\) and \(t\), let’s have a rule for forming new types—the one in (1).
For example, since \(e\) and \(t\) are types, then by (1), so is \((e → t)\). But now that \((e → t)\) is a type, (1) may apply again: we can use \(e\), \(t\), and \((e → t)\) to form a bunch more types, like \((e → (e → t))\), \(((e → t) → t)\), \((e → (e → (e → t)))\), and an infinite number more…

