It’s example time.

Example 1
For the first example let’s show that \(⌜537⌝\) is an \(exp\) (and that it has a particular interpretation in \(\mathcal{M}_{ANA}\)).
\[\begin{prooftree}
\AxiomC{$⟨⌜5⌝, I_{ANA}(⌜5⌝)⟩ ⊢ atom$}
\RightLabel{Rule 2}\UnaryInfC{$⟨⌜5⌝, I_{ANA}(⌜5⌝)⟩ ⊢ n$}
\AxiomC{$⟨⌜3⌝, I_{ANA}(⌜3⌝)⟩ ⊢ atom$}
\RightLabel{Rule 2}\UnaryInfC{$⟨⌜3⌝, I_{ANA}(⌜3⌝)⟩ ⊢ n$}
\AxiomC{$⟨⌜7⌝, I_{ANA}(⌜7⌝)⟩ ⊢ atom$}
\RightLabel{Rule 2}\UnaryInfC{$⟨⌜7⌝, I_{ANA}(⌜7⌝)⟩ ⊢ n$}
\RightLabel{Rule 2}\BinaryInfC{$⟨⌜37⌝, I_{ANA}(⌜3⌝) * 10^{length(⌜7⌝)} + I_{ANA}(⌜7⌝)⟩ ⊢ n$}
\RightLabel{Rule 2}\BinaryInfC{$⟨⌜537⌝, I_{ANA}(⌜5⌝) * 10^{length(⌜37⌝)} + I_{ANA}(⌜3⌝) * 10^{length(⌜7⌝)} + I_{ANA}(⌜7⌝)⟩ ⊢ n$}
\RightLabel{Rule 3}\UnaryInfC{$⟨⌜537⌝, I_{ANA}(⌜5⌝) * 10^{length(⌜37⌝)} + I_{ANA}(⌜3⌝) * 10^{length(⌜7⌝)} + I_{ANA}(⌜7⌝)⟩ ⊢ exp$}
\end{prooftree}\]
What’s proved is the statement in the very last line of this derivation: that the string \(⌜537⌝\) has the interpretation \[I_{ANA}(⌜5⌝) * 10^{length(⌜37⌝)} + I_{ANA}(⌜3⌝) * 10^{length(⌜7⌝)} + I_{ANA}(⌜7⌝)\] and the category \(exp\). Since we know that

\(I_{ANA}(⌜5⌝) = 5\),
\(I_{ANA}(⌜3⌝) = 3\), and
\(I_{ANA}(⌜7⌝) = 7\)

as well as what \(length(⌜37⌝)\) and \(length(⌜7⌝)\) are—\(2\) and \(1\), respectively—the interpretation in the very last line of this proof/derivation can be rewritten as
\[5 * 10^{2} + 3 * 10^{1} + 7\]
which, of course, is \(537\). Thus the full judgment ultimately simplifies to:
\[⟨⌜537⌝, 537⟩ ⊢ exp\]


Example 2
For the second example, we’ll show that \(⌜((37 + 1) × 0) = 5⌝\) is an \(s\), and also what its interpretation in \(\mathcal{M}_{ANA}\) is.
\[\begin{prooftree}
\AxiomC{$⟨⌜3⌝, I_{ANA}(⌜3⌝)⟩ ⊢ atom$}
\RightLabel{Rule 2}\UnaryInfC{$⟨⌜3⌝, I_{ANA}(⌜3⌝)⟩ ⊢ n$}
\AxiomC{$⟨⌜7⌝, I_{ANA}(⌜7⌝)⟩ ⊢ atom$}
\RightLabel{Rule 2}\UnaryInfC{$⟨⌜7⌝, I_{ANA}(⌜7⌝)⟩ ⊢ n$}
\RightLabel{Rule 2}\BinaryInfC{$⟨⌜37⌝, I_{ANA}(⌜3⌝) * 10^{length(⌜7⌝)} + I_{ANA}(⌜7⌝)⟩ ⊢ n$}
\RightLabel{Rule 3}\UnaryInfC{$⟨⌜37⌝, I_{ANA}(⌜3⌝) * 10^{length(⌜7⌝)} + I_{ANA}(⌜7⌝)⟩ ⊢ exp$}
\AxiomC{$⟨⌜1⌝, I_{ANA}(⌜1⌝)⟩ ⊢ atom$}
\RightLabel{Rule 2}\UnaryInfC{$⟨⌜1⌝, I_{ANA}(⌜1⌝)⟩ ⊢ n$}
\RightLabel{Rule 3}\UnaryInfC{$⟨⌜1⌝, I_{ANA}(⌜1⌝)⟩ ⊢ exp$}
\RightLabel{Rule 3}\BinaryInfC{$⟨⌜(37 + 1)⌝, I_{ANA}(⌜3⌝) * 10^{length(⌜7⌝)} + I_{ANA}(⌜7⌝) + I_{ANA}(⌜1⌝)⟩ ⊢ exp$}
\AxiomC{$⟨⌜0⌝, I_{ANA}(⌜0⌝)⟩ ⊢ atom$}
\RightLabel{Rule 2}\UnaryInfC{$⟨⌜0⌝, I_{ANA}(⌜0⌝)⟩ ⊢ n$}
\RightLabel{Rule 3}\UnaryInfC{$⟨⌜0⌝, I_{ANA}(⌜0⌝)⟩ ⊢ exp$}
\RightLabel{Rule 3}\BinaryInfC{$⟨⌜((37 + 1) × 0)⌝, (I_{ANA}(⌜3⌝) * 10^{length(⌜7⌝)} + I_{ANA}(⌜7⌝) + I_{ANA}(⌜1⌝)) * I_{ANA}(⌜0⌝)⟩ ⊢ exp$}
\AxiomC{$⟨⌜5⌝, I_{ANA}(⌜5⌝)⟩ ⊢ atom$}
\RightLabel{Rule 2}\UnaryInfC{$⟨⌜5⌝, I_{ANA}(⌜5⌝)⟩ ⊢ n$}
\RightLabel{Rule 3}\UnaryInfC{$⟨⌜5⌝, I_{ANA}(⌜5⌝)⟩ ⊢ exp$}
\RightLabel{Rule 4}\BinaryInfC{$⟨⌜((37 + 1) × 0) = 5⌝, (I_{ANA}(⌜3⌝) * 10^{length(⌜7⌝)} + I_{ANA}(⌜7⌝) + I_{ANA}(⌜1⌝)) * I_{ANA}(⌜0⌝) = I_{ANA}(⌜5⌝)⟩ ⊢ s$}
\end{prooftree}\]
Indeed, we’ve proved that \(⌜((37 + 1) × 0) = 5⌝\) is an \(s\). What we’ve also shown is that the interpretation of this \(s\) is \(\True\) just in case
\[(I_{ANA}(⌜3⌝) * 10^{length(⌜7⌝)} + I_{ANA}(⌜7⌝) + I_{ANA}(⌜1⌝)) * I_{ANA}(⌜0⌝)\]
is equal to \(I_{ANA}(⌜5⌝)\).
In fact, since we know that \[I_{ANA}(⌜0⌝) = 0\] and that multiplying anything by \(0\) gives you \(0\), we can determine that
\[(I_{ANA}(⌜3⌝) * 10^{length(⌜7⌝)} + I_{ANA}(⌜7⌝) + I_{ANA}(⌜1⌝)) * I_{ANA}(⌜0⌝)\]
is equal to \(0\) without even worrying about what
\[I_{ANA}(⌜3⌝) * 10^{length(⌜7⌝)} + I_{ANA}(⌜7⌝) + I_{ANA}(⌜1⌝)\]
evaluates to. Though, if we were to evaluate it, we’d of course find that it evaluates to \(38\).
Since \(I_{ANA}(⌜5⌝)\) is \(5\), the interpretation of this sentence is \(\True\) if and only if \(0\) and \(5\) are the same number. They’re not, so the interpretation is \(\False\).

