We can also assign a lexical entry to the preposition to, according to which it is interpreted as the identity function \((λx.x)\). This interpretation for the preposition makes it semantically vacuous: it simply takes an entity argument (\(x\)) and spits it right back out.

\(⟨\textit{to}, (λx.x)⟩ ⊢ (pp/np)\)

Recall that the semantic type of prepositional phrases (\(pp\)) is \(e\). As a result, the semantic type of to is \((e → e)\).
According to the first lexical entry for showed, the verb combines with an object noun phrase; then it combines with a prepositional phrase; and then it combines with a subject noun phrase, yielding, e.g., (1-a). According to the second, it combines with an indirect-object noun phrase; then a direct-object noun phrase; and then the subject, yielding, e.g., (1-b).
